<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/laconia-mills.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/laconia-mills-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/laconia-mills.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/new-hampshire-cities/">Cities</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Laconia</span></nav><p class="kicker">Lakes Region · Belknap County</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser in Laconia, NH</h1><p class="lede">Laconia sits between Lake Winnipesaukee and Lake Winnisquam, and a lot of its property sits empty for part of the year. That is where the claims start: frozen pipes, snow-loaded roofs and damage nobody saw for weeks.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>County</small><b>Belknap County</b></li><li><small>Region</small><b>Lakes Region</b></li><li><small>Housing</small><b>lake homes, seasonal camps and older in-town houses</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="local-card"><div><h2 class="local-t">At a glance</h2><p><b>Areas we cover:</b> Weirs Beach, Lakeport, downtown Laconia, Paugus Bay, the Lake Opechee shore.</p><p><b>Storm history:</b> The January 1998 ice storm hit the Lakes Region hard, bringing down trees and power lines around Laconia and leaving many homes and camps without heat for days.</p></div><ul class="chips"><li>Frozen pipes in camps</li><li>Ice storms</li><li>Roof snow load</li><li>Falling trees</li><li>Dock &amp; boathouse damage</li></ul></div>

<h2>Between the lakes</h2>
<p>Laconia wraps around Paugus Bay and Lake Opechee, with Winnipesaukee to the northeast and Winnisquam to the southwest. Weirs Beach is the summer side of town – boardwalk, marinas, cottages and condos. Lakeport and downtown hold older year-round homes, mill-era buildings and apartments. Around the shorelines, seasonal camps sit next to big year-round lake houses built or rebuilt in the last few decades.</p>
<p>That split between summer and year-round property shapes nearly every claim we see from Laconia.</p>
<h2>The empty-camp problem</h2>
<p>A camp closed for the winter is the easiest place in New Hampshire for a pipe to freeze. If a system wasn’t fully drained, or the heat failed during an outage, water can run for weeks before anyone notices. By spring, the damage includes floors, walls, cabinets, mold and sometimes the foundation.</p>
<p>Two things usually decide these claims:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Coverage conditions.</strong> Many policies limit freeze coverage in vacant or unoccupied homes unless you maintained heat or shut off and drained the water. Read yours before you close up. If coverage is disputed, talk to a New Hampshire attorney.</li>
<li><strong>Scope and price.</strong> Once coverage is accepted, the argument is about how much needs to be replaced. Long-standing water losses often need more tear-out and drying than a first estimate allows.</li>
</ol>
<p>See <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/pipe-burst-water-appraisal/">burst-pipe appraisal</a> for more.</p>
<h2>Lake houses, docks and boathouses</h2>
<p>Larger lake homes come with custom finishes, big glass and complex rooflines – all expensive to repair and easy to under-scope. Docks, boathouses and other structures on the water may be covered under an “other structures” limit, a separate endorsement or not at all, depending on the policy. Ice movement and storms both damage them. Know where they sit in your policy before you need it.</p>
<h2>Snow, ice and trees</h2>
<p>The Lakes Region gets more snow than the southern tier, and seasonal camps often go all winter without anyone clearing the roof. Older camps were not built for heavy loads, and collapse claims follow big winters. Ice storms add falling trees to the list; many lake lots are thick with pines right up to the house. Our <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/ice-dam-roof-snow-appraisal/">roof snow appraisal</a> and <a href="/nh-appraisal-services/falling-tree-claim-appraisal/">falling-tree appraisal</a> pages explain how we evaluate these.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Closing up for winter</b>Write down the date you drained the plumbing and set the heat, and keep receipts from whoever did it. If there’s a claim later, that record helps.</div>
<p>In-town Laconia is its own market. Lakeport and the streets near downtown hold older two-families and single-family homes, many with the same aging roofs, plaster walls and wood heat you see elsewhere in central New Hampshire. Those owners deal with the usual ice-dam and pipe claims rather than lake-specific ones, and their estimates deserve the same careful scope.</p>
<h2>Laconia and the Lakes Region</h2>
<p>New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser serves Laconia, Gilford, Meredith and the rest of the Lakes Region. We review estimates, represent policyholders in appraisal and serve as umpire. Check timing with the <a href="/nh-claim-calculators/nh-claim-deadline-calendar/">claim deadline calendar</a>, or <a href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">contact us</a>.</p>

<h2>Appraisal help available in Laconia</h2>
<p>Every New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser service is available in Laconia and the surrounding towns, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/appraisal-clause-representation-nh/">Policyholder’s Appraiser on the Panel</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/homeowners-claim-appraisal-nh/">Single-Family Home Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/nh-commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Condo, Association &amp; Business Claims</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/roof-and-hail-appraisal/">Roof Damage &amp; Hail Disputes</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/wind-microburst-tornado-appraisal/">Microburst &amp; Windstorm Losses</a></li><li><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/tropical-storm-hurricane-appraisal-nh/">Tropical Remnants &amp; Coastal Storms</a></li></ul>
<h2>Asked by Laconia owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My camp pipes froze while it was closed for the winter. Is it covered?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on your policy’s freeze and vacancy language. Many policies require that you maintained heat or drained the system. Read the wording, and ask an attorney if coverage is denied.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is my dock covered by my homeowners policy?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Sometimes, under the other-structures limit; sometimes only by endorsement. Check your policy and declarations page.</p></div></details></div>
